 Allows developers to manage runtime errors gracefully, ensuring the normal flow of a program continues instead of crashing abruptly.

Exception: An object representing an error or unexpected event, usually a subclass of java.lang.Exception.
Checked Exceptions: Must be either caught or declared in the method signature (e.g., IOException).
Unchecked Exceptions: Runtime exceptions that don’t require explicit handling (e.g., NullPointerException, ArithmeticException).
Error: Serious issues (like OutOfMemoryError) that applications typically should not try to handle.


try	Defines a block of code to monitor for exceptions.
catch	Handles the exception if one occurs in the try block.
finally	Executes code regardless of whether an exception occurred (e.g., cleanup).
throw	Used to explicitly throw an exception.
throws	Declares exceptions a method might throw.

public class Example { public static void main(String[] args) { 
try { int result = 10 / 0; System.out.println("Result: " + result); }
catch (ArithmeticException e) { System.out.println("Error: Division by zero!"); } 
finally { System.out.println("Execution completed."); } } }

Errors and Exceptions are subclasses of the Throwable class in Java


